#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# rescale

#% text
# `rescale(x, [a, b])` gives `(x - a)/(b - a)`, mapping `[a, b]` onto `[0, 1]`.

#% text
# `rescale(x, [a, b], [c, d])` maps `[a, b]` onto `[c, d]`.

#% text
# `rescale(xs)` maps the list `xs` onto `[0, 1]` by its own range.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Values outside `[a, b]` are mapped linearly too, outside the target range.

#% item
# Symbols are allowed: the result is the linear expression.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
rescale(5, [0, 10])
#% output
#= 1/2

#% cell
rescale(5, [0, 10], [-1, 1])
#% output
#= 0

#% cell
rescale([2, 4, 6, 10])
#% output
#= [0, 1/4, 1/2, 1]

#% cell
rescale(x, [a, b])
#% output
#= (x - a)/(b - a)

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# Celsius to Fahrenheit, mapping `[0, 100]` onto `[32, 212]`:

#% cell
rescale(37, [0, 100], [32, 212])
#% output
#= 493/5

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The result of `rescale(xs)` lies in `[0, 1]`:

#% cell
r = rescale([3.5, -1.0, 2.0, 8.0]); [min(r), max(r)]
#% output
#= [0.0, 1.0]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `clip`, `min`, `max`
